# log4j-core's message formatting asks Interpolator to resolve each `${prefix:name}`
# variable: it reads the handler registered for the prefix from a map held at the
# StrLookup interface type and calls `lookup(name)` on it. The jndi handler
# (JndiLookup) hands the variable text it is given to JndiManager.lookup(String),
# which passed it straight to javax.naming Context.lookup(String) -- so a name
# carrying its own scheme and authority ("ldap://host/a") made the JVM fetch and
# instantiate whatever a directory server outside the caller's control described.
# The restriction that shipped for this weakness (log4j-core/.../net/JndiManager.java)
# keeps that call and gates it: it parses `new URI(name)`, returns null unless the
# scheme is in an allowed-protocol list, and for ldap/ldaps additionally requires
# the host to be a local IP and the entry's javaClassName to be one of eight JDK
# value classes, rejecting javaReferenceAddress/javaFactory entries outright.
#
# javax.naming Context.lookup carries the code.Deserialization label -- the concept
# the readObject/Yaml.load/XMLDecoder sink family carries, since a naming service
# outside the caller's control chooses what object gets instantiated. The expect
# case holds the whole real path: an untrusted header value enters the
# application's log call, the resolver's prefix registry hands back a handler held
# at the interface type, and the dispatch on that interface-typed receiver reaches
# the implementor's body, whose own indirection (convertJndiName, the manager's
# lookup) ends at Context.lookup's argument. A call on an interface-typed receiver
# routes to the implementors' bodies as reach-only targets: the arguments flow in
# so a sink inside an implementor is coverable, while the call's own result keeps
# the unresolved conservative semantics, so an implementor's shared return node
# cannot merge taint from one call site into another.
#
# The reject case is the flow that remains once the resolver cannot be handed
# variable text from a request: the resolver still exists and is still called on
# every format, but only the configuration's own constant property name reaches
# it, and the request-derived text is appended verbatim. That is a flow
# distinction rather than a control one, because restricting the name instead of
# removing the flow is not something this engine credits, and should not be
# credited here even if it could: the allowlist gate above is a branch-sensitive
# `return null` on a value derived from the same tainted variable (the
# sanitize-or-return idiom the dataflow design records as out of scope for a
# branch-insensitive solver), and it is itself bypassable -- the following release
# removed the message-side lookup rather than narrowing the allowlist further. A
# control meaning only "a name is checked against a list" would endorse exactly
# the hole that restriction left open. Measured on the two real revisions of
# JndiManager.java plus JndiLookup.java: VYQL-DESER-001 labels the Context.lookup
# argument on both, so the restriction is not a discriminator.
#
# The last three cases isolate the dispatch construct itself, and deliberately
# not on a lookup sink: the javax.naming sink pattern matches the bare method
# name `lookup` whenever the receiver's type cannot be proven (recv_type unknown
# is treated as "cannot disprove"), so the registry call would be labelled a sink
# itself and hide the hop. With Runtime.exec instead, the pair differs in the
# receiver's declared type alone -- the same registry read, the same single
# implementation, the same argument -- and both carry taint into the
# implementation's body: a receiver of a concrete class type through its own
# method, a receiver of the interface type through the implementor's body. The
# final case holds the boundary of that routing: a method with the same name on
# a class that does not implement the interface receives nothing, because the
# dispatch is driven by the type hierarchy, not by the method name.
#
# verify_current_expected: fixed, the interface-typed dispatch reaches the
# implementor's body.

test "rank1339: a registry dispatch on an interface-typed handler carries variable text to Context.lookup"
  lang java
  file JndiLookup.java
  expect VYQL-DESER-001
  code
  ```
  import java.util.HashMap;
  import java.util.Map;
  import java.util.Objects;

  import javax.naming.Context;
  import javax.naming.InitialContext;
  import javax.naming.NamingException;

  interface StrLookup {
    String lookup(String name) throws NamingException;
  }

  class JndiManager {
    private final Context context;

    private JndiManager(final Context context) {
      this.context = context;
    }

    public static JndiManager getDefaultManager() throws NamingException {
      return new JndiManager(new InitialContext());
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <T> T lookup(final String name) throws NamingException {
      return (T) this.context.lookup(name);
    }
  }

  class JndiLookup implements StrLookup {
    static final String CONTAINER_JNDI_RESOURCE_PATH_PREFIX = "java:comp/env/";

    public String lookup(final String key) throws NamingException {
      if (key == null) {
        return null;
      }
      final String jndiName = convertJndiName(key);
      final JndiManager jndiManager = JndiManager.getDefaultManager();
      return Objects.toString(jndiManager.lookup(jndiName), null);
    }

    private String convertJndiName(final String jndiName) {
      if (!jndiName.startsWith(CONTAINER_JNDI_RESOURCE_PATH_PREFIX) && jndiName.indexOf(':') == -1) {
        return CONTAINER_JNDI_RESOURCE_PATH_PREFIX + jndiName;
      }
      return jndiName;
    }
  }

  class DateLookup implements StrLookup {
    public String lookup(final String name) {
      return "2021-12-10";
    }
  }

  class Interpolator {
    private final Map<String, StrLookup> strLookupMap = new HashMap<>();

    Interpolator() {
      strLookupMap.put("jndi", new JndiLookup());
      strLookupMap.put("date", new DateLookup());
    }

    public String lookup(final String var) throws NamingException {
      final int prefixPos = var.indexOf(':');
      if (prefixPos < 0) {
        return null;
      }
      final String prefix = var.substring(0, prefixPos).toLowerCase();
      final String name = var.substring(prefixPos + 1);
      final StrLookup lookup = strLookupMap.get(prefix);
      if (lookup != null) {
        return lookup.lookup(name);
      }
      return null;
    }
  }

  class VersionServlet extends HttpServlet {
    private final Interpolator resolver = new Interpolator();

    public void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, StringBuilder toAppendTo) throws NamingException {
      final String message = request.getHeader("X-Api-Version");
      toAppendTo.append(resolver.lookup(message));
    }
  }

  class HttpServlet {}
  interface HttpServletRequest { String getHeader(String name); }
  ```

test "rank1339: a resolver reached only by a configured property name, with request text appended verbatim"
